Stacks Theory
==============

Core Idea:
- Last-In, First-Out (LIFO) data structure
- Push adds to top, Pop removes from top
- Only the top element is accessible at any time
- Think of it as a stack of plates -- you can only take the top one


When to Use Stacks:
--------------------
- Function call management -- tracking nested function calls and returns (the call stack)
- Undo/redo functionality -- each action is pushed, undo pops the last one
- Expression evaluation -- parsing and evaluating math expressions, postfix/prefix notation
- Balanced parentheses/bracket matching -- push opening, pop and compare on closing
- Backtracking algorithms -- DFS in graphs/trees, maze solving, explore a path and back up
- Browser history -- back button behavior (most recent page first)
- String reversal -- push all chars, pop them out in reverse
- Syntax parsing / compilers -- tracking nested scopes, tokens, grammar rules
- Removing adjacent duplicates -- push chars, pop when top matches current (chain reactions)


When NOT to Use Stacks:
------------------------
- Need FIFO (First-In, First-Out) order -- use a queue (e.g., task scheduling, BFS)
- Need random access by index -- use an array or list
- Need to access both ends frequently -- use a deque
- Need sorted order or priority-based access -- use a priority queue / heap
- Need fast lookups by key -- use a hash map
- Need to process elements in insertion order without reversing -- queue or linked list
- Data doesn't have a nesting or depth-based relationship -- stack adds unnecessary complexity


Simple Test:
------------
Ask yourself:
  "Does this problem involve nesting, reversing, backtracking,
   or undoing the most recent action?"

  YES -> Stack is likely the right choice
  NO  -> Think queue, deque, hashmap, or array


Common Stack Patterns:
-----------------------

1. Matching / Balancing
   - Valid parentheses, HTML tag matching
   - Push opening, pop and compare on closing

2. Monotonic Stack
   - Next greater/smaller element
   - Maintain increasing or decreasing order on the stack
   - Used for: stock span, temperatures, histogram problems

3. Stack + Count / Stack with Pairs (tuple stack)
   - Instead of pushing raw elements, push (element, count) tuples
   - Core idea: collapse consecutive duplicates into a single entry with a count
   - How it works:
       -> If top of stack has same element, increment its count
       -> If count reaches k (or target), pop it off
       -> Otherwise, push (element, 1) as a new entry
   - Why it's powerful:
       -> Handles chain reactions naturally -- after popping, the new top
          might now match the next incoming element
       -> Avoids re-scanning the string/array after removals
   - When to use:
       -> "Remove k adjacent duplicates" style problems
       -> Run-length encoding / decoding
       -> Any problem where consecutive same-elements need to be grouped or removed
   - Example problems:
       -> Remove All Adjacent Duplicates in String II (LeetCode 1209)
       -> Remove All Adjacent Duplicates in String (LeetCode 1047)
       -> Decoded String at Index with counting

4. Expression Evaluation
   - Infix to postfix conversion
   - Evaluate postfix/prefix expressions
   - Calculator problems (basic, with nested parentheses)

5. DFS Simulation
   - Iterative DFS using explicit stack instead of recursion
   - Tree/graph traversal without call stack overflow


Quick Reference:
----------------
Situation                                  |  Approach
------------------------------------------|--------------------
Matching brackets/parentheses              |  Stack
Next greater/smaller element               |  Monotonic stack
Remove adjacent duplicates                 |  Stack (or stack + count)
Evaluate expression                        |  Stack
Iterative DFS                              |  Stack
Undo last action                           |  Stack
Need FIFO order                            |  Queue (not stack)
Need both ends                             |  Deque (not stack)
Need min/max while using stack             |  Min stack (two stacks)
